    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A10392A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 899 of the laws of 1984, relat-\ning to the creation of the Roosevelt Island operating corporation, in\nrelation to the management of such corporation\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To improve the governance of the Roose-\nvelt Island Operating Corporation, in order to make it more represen-\ntative of Island residents,\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: -reconstitutes the RIOC board into a\nnine member body, with the commissioner of the Division of Housing and\nCommunity Renewal (DHCR, and the NYS Budget Director serving as ex-offi-\ncio members, and seven public members appointed by the governor subject\nto the advice and consent of the senate-one upon the recommendation of\nthe local member of the state senate, one upon the recommendation of the\nlocal member of the state assembly, and two upon the recommendation of\nthe mayor of the city of New York, all seven public members being resi-\ndents of Roosevelt Island;\n\n-provides that the elected public officials who represent Roosevelt\nIsland shall be provided with all information received by board members\nfor all board meeting agenda items;\n\n-provides that all meetings of the board of directors or any committee\nof the board shall be subject to the open meetings law;\n\n-requires purchase and contracts to be entered into in accordance with a\nprogram adopted under 52879 of the public authorities law;\n\n-provides that prior to hiring the chief executive officer of the corpo-\nration, the board shall hold a public hearing to interview at least\nthree qualified candidates for the position where at residents of Roose-\nvelt Island can question the candidates;, and\n\n-requires that any expenditures of the corporation be in accordance with\nthe adopted budget or a modification approved by the board of directors.\n\n-act to take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have become a\nlaw.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: RIOC was created in 1984 to manage Roosevelt Island,\noversee its continued development through the Master Plan and allocate\nstate capital funding and operational support. In recent years however,\nstate assistance has dwindled and the Island has had to become more\nself-sufficient. At the same time, residents have become increasingly\ndisenchanted with RIOC's governance. This bill would make RIOC more\nrepresentative of Island residents by reconstituting the RIOC board and\nrequiring that all of its public members be residents of the Island. It\nalso improves the board's accountability by providing for some of its\npublic members to be appointed upon the recommendation of the local\nelected officials who represent the Island. These elected officials are\n\nfamiliar with the Island and the unique and complex issues surrounding\nits governance, and they are, of necessity, responsive to the concerns\nexpressed by Island residents.\n\nThe legislation also improves the board's governance and transparency by\nrequiring a more robust search process prior to the hiring of a chief\nexecutive officer, by specifying that meetings of the board or any\ncommittee of the board are subject to the open meetings law, by requir-\ning that the corporation's expenditures be made in accordance with the\nbudget adopted by the board (or any modifications made by the board to\nthe budget), and by requiring-that purchases and contracts made by the\ncorporation be in accordance with the requirements of S2879 of the\npublic authorities law.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: None\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  To be determined\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Act to take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall\nhave become a law.\n\n",
